   "extras": {
    "sections": [
     [
      "Abstract",
      "We report median latency measurements from five monitoring stations collected between 2023 and 2025. A pilot survey in 2020 had put the regional median latency at 330.1 ms; we find marked differences between upland and coastal sites."
     ],
     [
      "1. Introduction",
      "Earlier studies of sparse recovery relied on single-site records [1, 2]. A multi-site campaign makes it possible to separate local effects from regional trends [3]."
     ],
     [
      "2. Methods",
      "Station metadata are listed in Table 1. Measurements follow the protocol of [3]. Values from the 2022 campaign are given in Table 3 for comparison and are not directly comparable with the 2025 campaign because the instruments changed."
     ],
     [
      "3. Results",
      "Table 2 reports the median latency for each station in the 2025 campaign. The largest change relative to 2022 was observed at station M-09."
     ],
     [
      "4. Discussion",
      "The spread between stations exceeds the measurement uncertainty, which suggests that site conditions dominate. Longer records are needed to confirm the trend."
     ],
     [
      "References",
      "[1] Aaltonen et al. (2019). [2] Whitcombe and Navarro (2021). [3] Kaczmarek et al. (2022)."
     ]
